<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi>Item Names

When referring to items by name, to <ansi fg="command">use</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">get</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">drop</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">read</ansi>, etc you may use
partial names to refer to items. For example, "<ansi fg="item">a heavy sword</ansi>" can be
referred to as "<ansi fg="item">sword</ansi>", or "<ansi fg="item">heavy</ansi>". You can also refer to it by
its full name: "<ansi fg="item">heavy sword</ansi>".

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Trinkets you find ━━━</ansi>

The small trinkets you sometimes find while searching each have their
own name. You can use any word of it, or several words in order:
"<ansi fg="item">Small Child's Doll</ansi>" answers to "<ansi fg="item">doll</ansi>", "<ansi fg="item">child's doll</ansi>" or
"<ansi fg="item">small doll</ansi>". Every one of them also answers to "<ansi fg="item">trinket</ansi>" and
"<ansi fg="item">bauble</ansi>", so <ansi fg="command">sell all bauble</ansi> sells them all.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Disambiguation ━━━</ansi>

Sometimes you may find yourself referring to items that share common
naming. For example, imagine these three items in your backpack, or
on the ground:

   <ansi fg="item">a heavy axe</ansi>, <ansi fg="item">a heavy sword</ansi>, <ansi fg="item">a heavy car axel</ansi>

Referring to "axe" or "heavy" is ambiguous. You can target a specific
item using either of these formats:

::rows
  <ansi fg="command">get 2.heavy</ansi>    Diku-style — number before the name.
  <ansi fg="command">get heavy#2</ansi>    Hash-style — number after the name.
::

Both would get the second match: the <ansi fg="item">heavy sword</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Targeting All ━━━</ansi>

Use <ansi fg="command">all</ansi> to affect every item on the ground, or
<ansi fg="command">all.<name></ansi> to affect every item matching a name:

::rows
  <ansi fg="command">get all</ansi>           Pick up everything on the ground.
  <ansi fg="command">drop all.potion</ansi>   Drop all potions from your backpack.
::

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Backpack vs. Equipped ━━━</ansi>

Commands like <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">identify</ansi> search both your
backpack and equipped items as a single pool. If you have a
dagger in your backpack and another wielded:

::rows
  <ansi fg="command">look dagger</ansi>      Looks at the first (backpack) dagger.
  <ansi fg="command">look 2.dagger</ansi>    Looks at the second (wielded) dagger.
::

The game tells you whether the item is in your backpack or
equipped when you examine it.

::rows
